  logged is the login form, which handles a password. Whatever we build needs a redaction rule baked in, not bolted on.

  First question — this is the fork that determines the whole architecture:

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether logs stay on the user's machine or get shipped to a server you can read. Everything else (levels,
  format, module shape) is downstream of this.

  - [1mConsole-only, structured[0m — a [38;5;153mlogger[39m module wrapping [38;5;153mconsole[39m with levels and a redaction pass. Zero infrastructure,
    zero privacy surface, works today. But it only helps when you can reproduce the issue or talk a user through opening
    devtools. Does not actually solve "debug production issues" for users you can't reach. Cheap to extend into remote
    shipping later if the module boundary is right.
  - [1mShip to a remote endpoint (self-built)[0m — same logger module, plus a batching transport that POSTs to a collector you
    run. Actually gives you production visibility. Costs: you need an endpoint that exists, a privacy/retention
    position, and failure handling for when the collector is down. Hard to undo once users' data is in flight.
  - [1mThird-party service (Sentry or similar)[0m — an SDK gives you remote errors, stack traces, and session context for very
    little code. Costs: a runtime dependency, a vendor holding user data, and it currently conflicts with this repo's
    zero-dependency state.

  [1mMy recommendation: console-only structured logging now, with the transport as a named seam.[0m This repo has no
  collector, no test runner, and no stated privacy position — building remote shipping before any of those exist means
  guessing at three things at once. A logger module with one clean [38;5;153mtransport[39m hook gets you the levels, format, and
  redaction immediately, and makes adding remote shipping a contained follow-up rather than a rewrite. If you already
  have a collector or a Sentry account, say so and I'd recommend differently.
